    "source_sha256": "9900da42897bc6ec6a42cd6844c37f05ff0b6ae830723cb61d53b6d560572090",
    "note": "WS-5.2 (2026-08-30): CKM_SP800_108_COUNTER_KDF / CKM_SP800_108_FEEDBACK_KDF were already implemented (OpenSSL KBKDF-backed) but had never been tested against real vectors, and turned out to have three real bugs, all fixed alongside this evidence: (1) the caller-supplied fixed input was routed to OpenSSL's \"salt\"/label param instead of \"info\"/context, (2) OpenSSL's auto-appended L and 0x00-separator fields (use-l/use-separator, both default-on) were left enabled with nothing in this engine's dataParams model to correctly supply/suppress them, and (3) FEEDBACK_KDF silently ignored CK_SP800_108_COUNTER_FORMAT entirely (no \"r\" param was ever passed to OpenSSL, so any non-default counter width was wrong). Root-caused by directly reading providers/implementations/kdfs/kbkdf.c (openssl/openssl @master) and empirically confirmed byte-for-byte against real ACVP vectors via the openssl(1) kdf CLI's KBKDF provider before touching the C++. Documented, real, remaining limitation: OpenSSL's KBKDF hardwires counter-first placement \u2014 this engine can only produce ACVP's \"before fixed data\" counterLocation, never \"after fixed data\" or \"middle fixed data\", regardless of what order a caller's CK_PRF_DATA_PARAM array requests. Evidence below is restricted to \"before fixed data\" accordingly. \"double pipeline iteration\" kdfMode is a separate, unimplemented mechanism (CKM_SP800_108_DOUBLE_PIPELINE_KDF) and out of scope here. CMAC-TDES excluded (this engine has no 3DES-CMAC; CLAUDE.md scopes this fork to modern/retained algorithms only)."
  },
